From df08235515219cfd91fcc075a5a5712f193f5d20 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:41:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Node machine: the lean front writes the fact words' kernel's
 window, and the kernel reads it in place (#2850)

`measure-node-machine --shadow --front-window`, the first part of step 3 of
the one-core path. The piece before made the kernel's window as a copy of the
lean front's records, and the copy cost more than the kernel.

- The lean front writes each record's inputs for the kernel as it makes the
  record (NodeFront.window_*): its kind, its class from its operands'
  classes, its operands' distances, its entry, its position, a call's
  arguments. A literal's line is written with its record, at its record's
  number.
- A name's line is found by a pass over the chunk's records after the
  front's (node_facts_window_names): the names' places by a compare and a
  compress, a prefetch of each line, then the read; its scalar path is its
  twin.
- A chunk's end keeps a waiting value's records whole, in a row, where the
  arrays have room. A value without room keeps its place alone and is cut
  (BUSTER_NODE_FACTS_CLASS_CUT), for good, with every record made above it;
  its calls' arguments are not kept, so the list holds no more than the
  arrays hold records.
- The kernel reads the front's columns where they are
  (node_facts_window_run): over poison with every record compared with T's,
  over decoys, and timed. Each input is compared with the one made from T's
  columns and, where the copy gives the expression, with the copy's.
- The same lean front without the window runs on the same chunks. Its ticks
  are what the window's are read against, and its records must be the same.

No compile takes any of it.

On the 9700X, TSC ticks a record of the expressions T takes at reader time:
the kernel in place 15.6, the names' pass 4.6 and the front's rise 9.7 on
sqlite3.c, 29.9 in all against the piece's gate of 35, where the kernel with
the copy reads 46.4. Lua reads 38.4 (the copy 49.7) and musl 61.5 (the copy
55.2: the front writes the window for every record it makes, and a third of
musl's names are first uses). Every expression T takes there is read in
place, and nothing differs. -c sqlite3.c is unchanged.

Each of the 122 faults put into the new code fails the node machine's tests.
The campaign's first runs found that a cut call's arguments, kept at a
chunk's end, could pass the list's room: they are no longer kept, the most
the list held is counted, and a test of 2,000 waiting calls reads it.

Part of #2850.

